## mTLS Connection Map

```
Backend  ──mTLS──▶  MySQL       (backend presents client cert)
Backend  ──mTLS──▶  RabbitMQ    (backend presents client cert)
Backend  ──TLS───▶  Keystone    (server TLS only, token auth)
Frontend ──mTLS──▶  Backend     (frontend presents client cert)
Frontend ──mTLS──▶  RabbitMQ    (frontend presents client cert)
```

## Post-Deployment Verification

Run these from the **backend** machine:

```bash
# MySQL — TLS connection
openssl s_client -connect {{ hosts.mysql.fqdn }}:3306 -starttls mysql \
  -CAfile {{ base_path }}/ca/ca.pem

# RabbitMQ — mTLS connection
openssl s_client -connect {{ hosts.rabbitmq.fqdn }}:5671 \
  -CAfile {{ base_path }}/ca/ca.pem \
  -cert {{ base_path }}/backend/certs/client-cert.pem \
  -key {{ base_path }}/backend/certs/client.key.nopass

# Keystone — TLS connection
curl -v --cacert {{ base_path }}/ca/ca.pem \
  https://{{ hosts.keystone.fqdn }}:5000/v3
```

Run from the **frontend** machine:

```bash
# Backend — mTLS connection
curl -v --cacert {{ base_path }}/ca/ca.pem \
  --cert {{ base_path }}/frontend/certs/client-cert.pem \
  --key {{ base_path }}/frontend/certs/client.key.nopass \
  https://{{ hosts.backend.fqdn }}:8443/health

# RabbitMQ — mTLS connection
openssl s_client -connect {{ hosts.rabbitmq.fqdn }}:5671 \
  -CAfile {{ base_path }}/ca/ca.pem \
  -cert {{ base_path }}/frontend/certs/client-cert.pem \
  -key {{ base_path }}/frontend/certs/client.key.nopass
```

## Renewal

When certificates approach expiry (default: {{ cert_validity_days }} days):
1. Regenerate this bundle using the same template in SSL Certificate Manager
2. The CA remains the same ({{ ca_validity_days }}-day validity) — only leaf certs change
3. Re-run `distribute-certs.sh` and restart services
4. No truststore changes needed (same CA)

## Rollback

If issues occur after deployment:

1. **Stop** the affected service
2. **Restore** previous certificate files from backup:
   ```bash
   ssh <host> "cp -a {{ base_path }}/<role>.bak/* {{ base_path }}/<role>/"
   ```
3. **Restart** the service
4. **Investigate** using `scripts/verify-certs.sh`

**Tip:** Before deploying, back up existing certs:
```bash
{% for role, host in hosts.items() %}
ssh {{ host.fqdn }} "cp -a {{ base_path }}/{{ role }} {{ base_path }}/{{ role }}.bak 2>/dev/null || true"
{% endfor %}
```

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Likely Cause | Fix |
|---------|-------------|-----|
| `PKIX path building failed` | Truststore missing CA | Verify `{{ base_path }}/<role>/stores/truststore.p12` contains the CA cert |
| `Hostname not verified` | Connecting via wrong hostname | Use FQDN from this guide, not IP (unless IP is in SAN) |
| `certificate_required` | Client cert not presented | Check client-keystore.p12 path in application.yml |
| `handshake_failure` | TLS version mismatch | Ensure both sides support {{ cipher.protocols }} |
| `password was incorrect` | Wrong keystore password | Password is: `{{ ssl_passphrase }}` |
| MySQL `ERROR 2026` | Client not using SSL | Add `useSSL=true&requireSSL=true` to JDBC URL |
| RabbitMQ `unknown_ca` | Client doesn't trust server CA | Verify truststore contains `{{ base_path }}/ca/ca.pem` |
